      controllers). No register/memory editing (intvdebug has no
      register-write call, matching every other port's scope).
  [x] cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake: MinGW-w64 cross-compile toolchain
      (adapted from the MSX port's own, simplified since jzintv_core has
      no SDL2 dependency of its own) using a prebuilt SDL3 mingw devel
      archive (SDL3-devel-3.4.14-mingw.tar.gz from SDL's own GitHub
      releases) rather than building SDL3 from source.
  [x] core/CMakeLists.txt: added src/pads/pads_cgc_win32.c to
      JZINTV_CORE_SOURCES under `if(WIN32)` -- jzIntv's own pads_cgc.c
      calls pad_cgc_win32_init() unconditionally under #ifdef WIN32 with
      no weak-symbol fallback (unlike Linux/macOS's pad_cgc_linux_init,
      which IS __attribute__((weak)) specifically so CGC support can be
      omitted). Gated behind WIN32 so it is a no-op on Linux; re-verified
      by a clean Linux configure+build+ctest after this change (below).
  [x] Two real bugs caught before or via an actual Wine run:
        - dbg_window.c's refresh_regs() reused a 16-byte buffer for a
          flags string that needs up to 78 bytes (found by inspection,
          fixed with a separate properly-sized buffer before ever
          building).
        - A genuine buffer overflow, caught only by an ACTUAL WINE CRASH
          (not by inspection): refresh_stic()'s shared `rgba_scratch`
          static buffer was sized only for the cards-sheet render
          (32768 bytes) but reused for all four STIC renders, including
          the palette sheet (65536 bytes) and the BACKTAB (61440 bytes)
          -- both larger than the buffer. The overflow silently wrote
          pixel data past the end of the buffer into adjacent .bss,
          corrupting the unrelated static `g_dbg` pointer a few hundred
          bytes away. Wine's own SEH handler caught the resulting wild
          pointer dereference (a page fault reading a garbage 0xC8-byte-
          pattern address -- literally leaked pixel color bytes) inside
          intv_debugger_pretranslate() on the very next message-loop
          iteration after the debugger window opened, correctly
          identified via disassembly of the crash address plus targeted
          fprintf tracing through construction (which showed g_dbg still
          correct immediately after the first refresh, then corrupted a
          timer tick or two later -- consistent with per-frame pixel
          data varying which bytes got clobbered). Fixed by sizing the
          scratch buffer to the max of all four renders' byte counts.
          This bug is unique to the Windows port; the GNOME/KDE debugger
          windows do not share a single scratch buffer across renders.
  [x] Layout fix (user-reported, 2026-08-10): the keypad window's digit
      grid and action-button row were left-justified against each
      controller's origin while the direction disc was centered within
      the same 220px column -- visually lopsided. Fixed by centering all
      three rows (digits, actions, disc) within that column, matching
      the GNOME port's own gtk_widget_set_halign(..., GTK_ALIGN_CENTER)
      on each row.
  [x] VERIFIED under Wine (11.14) with a genuine MinGW-w64 cross-compiled
      .exe -- not just "should work": ran with WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON and
      confirmed via grim screenshots that (a) the main window renders the
      actual FujiNet config ROM boot screen ("FUJINET CONFIG INTV" /
      "CHECKING WIFI...") through the GDI StretchDIBits path with correct
      colors; (b) the debugger window (INTV_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1) renders the
      BACKTAB, MOB grid, GRAM/GROM card sheet, and palette strip
      correctly, and stays stable through many 100ms refresh-timer ticks
      after the scratch-buffer fix; (c) the keypad window
      (INTV_OPEN_KEYPAD=1) renders both controllers with correctly
      centered digit pads, action buttons, and direction discs; (d) all
      three windows run simultaneously without crashing. Also confirmed
      the ROM-less error path earlier (a real native MessageBoxA showing
      the expected "missing exec.bin/grom.bin" text with correctly-
      resolved Windows paths).
  [x] Clean-room re-verification of the ordinary Linux build/ directory
      (GNOME + KDE) after the two shared-file changes above
      (core/CMakeLists.txt's WIN32-gated pads_cgc_win32.c,
      CMakeLists.txt's Darwin-gated CMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET): fresh
      cmake -B build configure + full build + all 16 ctest cases passing
