M6 (cont'd) -- macOS (AppKit) frontend: WRITTEN, but UNVERIFIED -- unlike
      GNOME/KDE/Windows, macOS cannot be built or run at all in this Linux
      environment (no Wine-equivalent for Cocoa/AppKit), so none of the
      below has been compiled, let alone run. Do not treat this as tested
      the way GNOME/KDE/Windows were until it has actually been built and
      run on real macOS hardware or CI:
  [ ] frontends/macos/{main.m,AppDelegate.h/.m,DisplayView.h/.m,
      debugger/DebuggerWindow.h/.m,keypad/KeypadWindow.h/.m,
      Info.plist.in}: same layout/scope as the GNOME/KDE/Windows ports
      (CoreGraphics-drawn display letterboxed to a fixed 4:3, full
      keyboard mapping via virtual keyCodes for arrows/numpad/modifiers
      plus charactersIgnoringModifiers for the rest, an NSTabView
      debugger window with CPU/STIC tabs, a keypad window with both hand
      controllers). No Machine/Display/CPU radio menus (the Intellivision
      has one fixed configuration, unlike CoCo/ADAM/MSX); no register/
      memory editing or Load Symbols UI, matching GNOME/KDE/Windows'
      scope exactly.
  [ ] DisplayView.m uses a plain NSTimer (60Hz), not a CVDisplayLink-fed
      phase-lock like the CoCo/ADAM ports: intvsession has no
      notify_vsync call, matching the reasoning already established for
      the GNOME frame-clock callback and the Windows WM_TIMER.
  [ ] KeypadWindow.m's DiscView deliberately does NOT negate dy the way
      the GNOME/KDE/Windows disc widgets each must -- AppKit's default
      (non-flipped) NSView coordinate system already puts y increasing
      upward, the same convention intv_host.h's disc_codes numbering
      assumes, so negating here would reintroduce the exact N/S-inversion
      bug those three ports had to fix (see their own file-header
      comments). This is the one place the macOS port's logic correctly
      diverges from the other three rather than transposing verbatim --
      worth double-checking first if the disc ever misbehaves once
      actually built.
  [ ] DebuggerWindow.m's STIC scratch buffer is sized to the max of all
      four intvstic_render_* outputs (STIC_SCRATCH_BYTES) from the start,
      not just the cards sheet -- carrying forward the fix a real Wine
      crash forced in the Windows port (frontends/windows/debugger/
      dbg_window.c's own file comment has the full story) rather than
      reintroducing the same undersized-buffer bug here blind.
  [ ] frontends/macos/CMakeLists.txt (written earlier) references
      data/icons/fujinet-go-intv.icns, which does not exist yet (M7,
      below) -- the bundle icon will be missing until that lands, but
      this does not block the app itself from building or running.

M7 -- Packaging: DONE.
  [x] Icon: data/icons/src/fujinet-go-intv-foreground.png copied byte-
      identical from fujinet-go-coco-desktop (verified: same md5sum as
      CoCo/ADAM/Apple II's own copy -- this is the shared FujiNet mark
      every desktop product composites over its own background colour).
      tools/icons/make-icons.py renders it over dark green (#1E4912, per
      user note 2026-08-10) into every hicolor size, fujinet-go-intv.icns,
      and frontends/windows/app.ico (a new addition over the CoCo script:
      Pillow's ICO writer, 16x16 + 32x32). The CMake install rules and
      intv_icon_name() plumbing for GNOME/KDE already existed from M5/M6
      (written before this session's own scope) -- only the actual image
      files were missing.
  [x] build-aux/flatpak/online.fujinet.go.intv.{gnome,kde}.yml: adapted
      from the CoCo manifests (jzIntv archive instead of XRoar tarball;
      -DWITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF always, since the Mattel EXEC/GROM have no
      freely-redistributable substitute the way CoCo's HDB-DOS pair is).
      BOTH manifests actually built, installed, and RUN with
      flatpak-builder + flatpak run against the real org.gnome.Platform/49
      and org.kde.Platform/6.11 runtimes pulled from Flathub -- not just
      written: real sandboxed processes came up showing the expected
      "roms is missing exec.bin/grom.bin" message (this build has
      WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF, correctly). Both test installs were uninstalled
      afterward.
  [x] Real bug caught by the GNOME flatpak build (not present in the
      ordinary Linux build): frontends/gnome/CMakeLists.txt's link line
      put -lstdc++ before jzintv_core.a in the actual command CMake
      generated (a transitively-required PUBLIC dependency gets appended
      at the very end of the flattened link order); harmless under this
      machine's linker defaults, but a hard "DSO missing from command
      line" failure under the GNOME SDK's stricter --as-needed toolchain.
      Fixed by naming jzintv_core explicitly ahead of stdc++ in the link
      list.
  [x] Real drift caught by the same build: cmake/Dependencies.cmake's
      FUJINET_COMMIT pin (b939b7f...) was no longer reachable on GitHub --
      exactly the risk the original plan flagged ("the pinned commit may
      move under us"). Re-pinned to 2fae40fd322abcbf7fe702ea9cd4b309c0288767
      (the commit third_party/fujinet-firmware was already sitting at
      locally, confirmed to carry the Intellivision firmware files) in
      both cmake/Dependencies.cmake and both flatpak manifests. Re-verified
      inert on the ordinary Linux build (full reconfigure + build + all 19
      ctest cases).
  [x] Real gap caught while verifying build-aux/ was actually tracked:
      .gitignore's `build*/` pattern was silently swallowing build-aux/
      too (a real directory of committed source, not a build product) --
      it never showed as untracked, so the flatpak manifests and NSIS
      script could have been "written" and silently never committed. Fixed
      with an explicit `!build-aux/` negation, the same fix CoCo's own
      .gitignore already carries with the identical trap documented in a
      comment. Also added the missing `.flatpak-builder/`/`build-flatpak*/`
      /`repo/` ignores CoCo's .gitignore has.
  [x] build-aux/windows/installer.nsi: adapted from CoCo's (per-user
      install, no elevation, same MUI2 page flow). NOT run through
      makensis in this environment (no nsis package available, no sudo) --
      unlike the flatpak manifests, this is UNVERIFIED syntax, following a
      known-working template closely. The Windows release CI job
      (.github/workflows/release.yml) will be the first real makensis run.
  [x] .github/workflows/{linux,macos,windows,flatpak,release}.yml: adapted
      from CoCo's own. linux.yml's recipe (build SDL3 from source, GTK4/
      libadwaita/Qt6 apt packages, ctest) was not run end-to-end in this
      environment (no GitHub Actions runner available locally), but every
      individual piece it exercises -- the Linux build, both frontends,
      the full ctest suite, DEB/RPM/TGZ packaging via CPack, both flatpak
      manifests -- WAS independently verified locally in this session.
      macos.yml and windows.yml are genuinely UNTESTED beyond what the
      Wine smoke test and static file inspection already proved for
      Windows; macOS CI will be the very first time that frontend is
      built at all. windows.yml needs no TRE/libsystre packages (unlike
      CoCo's own, which portalib/sdsx.c requires) -- jzintv_core has no
      analog.
  [x] CPack (DEB/RPM/TGZ): the CMakeLists.txt-level config already existed
      from before this session (icon-install loop, CPACK_* variables,
      per-frontend CPACK_PACKAGE_NAME). Actually ran `cpack -G DEB` locally
      and confirmed a real .deb was produced (dpkg-shlibdeps unavailable in
      this environment, so dependencies are unresolved in this local
      artifact -- CI's ubuntu-latest runner has it, so the shipped
      packages will carry real dependency lists).

M11 -- ECS, Intellivoice, NTSC/PAL, and a Settings dialog for all four
frontends -- requested 2026-08-12:

  [x] tools/jzintv/roms/ecs.bin (24576 bytes, from the same jzIntv
      20200712 source release exec.bin/grom.bin came from) added
      alongside them; tools/jzintv/embed-roms.py's EXPECTED table and
      core/CMakeLists.txt's INTV_ROM_DIR_SRC list both gained the third
      entry. core/tests/no_embedded_roms.py picks it up automatically (it
      scans every .bin in the ROM directory, not a fixed list) -- no
      script change needed there. COMPLIANCE.md's EXEC/GROM section
      renamed and extended to cover ECS.
  [x] core/jzintv/intv_host.{h,c}: intv_host_opts gained ecs/ivoice/pal
      fields (INTV_HW_AUTO/OFF/ON tri-state for the first two, matching
      jzIntv's own ecs_enable/ivc_enable convention); intv_host_start's
      previously-fixed 5-element argv became a counted push_arg()
      appender emitting --ecs=/--voice=/--pal only when not AUTO/off. A
      user-forced ECS=On is pre-checked against a real 24576-byte
      <rom_dir>/ecs.bin (intv_host_has_ecs_rom) BEFORE any argv is built,
      refusing with -1 rather than letting jzIntv's own cfg_init() discover
      a missing image and exit(1) the whole process out from under the
      emulator thread -- confirmed this matters: an early version of
      hwopts_test (below) hit exactly this failure mode until
      core/CMakeLists.txt's ROM list actually included ecs.bin.
  [x] intv_pad_side widened with INTV_PAD_ECS_LEFT/_RIGHT (top bit selects
      intv.pad1 vs intv.pad0, same scan codes) -- intv_host_pad_key/_disc
      needed only an AND-with-2/AND-with-1 change, no new functions, so
      every existing 0/1 caller (all four keypad windows, pad_test.c) kept
      working unmodified. New intv_host_ecs_key/_ecs_keys_clear inject
      directly into intv.pad1.k[0..6]'s scan matrix, transposed
      entry-by-entry from the staged tree's cfg/mapping.c "KEYB_*" table
      (48 base keys + Shift/Ctrl/Esc; the upstream "<<8" shifted-symbol
      convenience entries -- KEYB_MINUS/EQUAL/QUOTE/etc -- deliberately
      NOT carried over, since real shifted characters work by holding
      Shift with the base key, exactly like the real hardware).
  [x] core/include/intvsession.h + core/src/session.c: intvsession_start
      now takes an intvsession_start_opts * (NULL = everything AUTO/NTSC);
      intvsession_default_opts reads it from the settings store ("ecs",
      "ivoice", "video_standard" keys); intvsession_has_ecs_rom mirrors
      but stays separate from intvsession_has_system_roms (a missing
      ecs.bin must not stop the machine from booting at all).
      intvsession_hw_mode_name/video_name are NULL-terminated label tables
      every frontend's Settings dialog walks rather than hardcoding
      option strings four times over. intvsession_pad_side widened to
      match intv_pad_side; intvsession_ecs_key_set/_ecs_keys_clear wrap
      the intv_host_ecs_key calls. Breaking change to intvsession_start's
      signature -- every one of the four frontends' call sites updated in
      the same pass (grep confirmed no stragglers).
  [x] core/src/intv_keymap.c: intvsession_ecs_key_from_keysym, a second
      pure function mirroring intvsession_key_from_keysym but sourced from
      cfg_key_bind[]'s column 3 ("ECS Keyboard setup") instead of column
      0. Needed three new non-printable keysym symbols
      (INTVSESSION_KEYSYM_ESCAPE/_RETURN/_BACKSPACE) the base map never
      needed. A session-wide "keyboard_mode" setting (0 = hand
      controllers, 1 = ECS keyboard) picks which of the two functions a
      frontend's key handler calls, since the host keyboard can't drive
      both at once.
  [x] core/src/gamepad_sdl.c: the hardcoded 2-side poll/dispatch loops
      became NUM_SIDES (4) -- pads 3/4 auto-assign to the ECS pair the
      same way pads 1/2 already assign to the base pair. No ECS-enabled
      check needed: driving intv.pad1 when ECS is off is harmless (nothing
      reads it off the peripheral bus).
  [x] All four frontends: a Settings/Preferences dialog (GNOME:
      AdwPreferencesDialog in a new prefs.c; KDE: a new SettingsDialog
      QDialog; macOS: inline in AppDelegate.m, Cmd-, in the app menu;
      Windows: inline WNDCLASSA in main.c, new IDM_SETTINGS/IDC_SET_*
      ids) -- ECS/Intellivoice/Video Standard apply by restarting the
      session when the dialog closes (each frontend already had, or
      gained, its own restart_session/-restartSession/
      intv_window_restart_session helper); the ECS Keyboard toggle applies
      live. Each frontend's key-forward path also gained the
      keyboard_mode branch and a focus-loss handler
      (GtkEventControllerFocus / QWidget::focusOutEvent /
      -resignFirstResponder / WM_KILLFOCUS) calling
      intvsession_ecs_keys_clear so a key held at the moment focus moves
      away doesn't stay stuck in the emulated matrix.
  [x] New tests: core/tests/hwopts_test.c (white-box cfg_t intv.ecs_enable/
      ivc_enable/pal_mode/stic.pal/speed.periph.min_tick assertions per
      option, one intv_host_start/stop cycle per case) and
      core/tests/ecs_key_test.c (intv_host_ecs_key bit-exact + chording +
      clear-all). Extended pad_test.c (ECS pair injection doesn't disturb
      pad0), session_test.c (settings round-trip + intvsession_default_opts
      + the forced-ECS-with-no-ROM refusal path), keymap_test.c (ECS
      keysym mapping), and gamepad_test.c (4-side intv_pad_for_port
      auto-assignment). VERIFIED: full ctest (14 suites, up from 12) green
      in both WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON and =OFF configs.
  [x] VERIFIED end to end: GNOME and KDE built and ctest-passed natively;
      Windows cross-built with mingw-w64 (build-win/) and ctest-passed
      under Wine (WINEPATH pointed at the mingw/SDL3 runtime DLLs). macOS
      is source-complete (AppDelegate.m Settings window, DisplayView.m ECS
      routing) but, as with the rest of that frontend, NOT built or run in
      this environment -- see README's own macOS caveat; first real
      compile will be macOS CI.
  [x] README.md's frontends paragraph and ctest suite count (12 -> 14)
      updated; COMPLIANCE.md's EXEC/GROM section extended to EXEC/GROM/ECS.

